WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:05.760
At long last, the "Black Widow" solo movie
has arrived and Natasha Romanoff is getting

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:07.660
her time in the spotlight.

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:12.720
In addition to potentially laying the groundwork
for future stories, the prequel also offers

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:16.610
a few sly winks at the MCU and Marvel Comics.

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:19.260
Here are some Easter eggs you missed in "Black
Widow."

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:25.480
When "Black Widow" moves to its present day
timeline of 2016, our title hero is on the

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:31.310
run from probably the busiest Secretary of
State in U.S. history, retired General Thaddeus

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:34.980
"Thunderbolt" Ross, who's convinced he's got
her pinned down.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:39.359
Natasha calls Ross on his cell, warning him
not to embarrass himself.

00:00:39.359 --> 00:00:44.750
It turns out that she's on a ferry in Norway,
which, at best, puts Ross six time zones and

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:46.339
a continent away.

00:00:46.339 --> 00:00:51.299
As Nat settles into her new home, courtesy
of her ally and supplier, Mason, she cracks

00:00:51.299 --> 00:00:57.339
a Norwegian beer and settles in to watch Roger
Moore do his thing as Agent 007; apparently

00:00:57.339 --> 00:01:01.369
she's a big enough James Bond fan to quote
the film line-by-line.

00:01:01.369 --> 00:01:06.300
This Easter egg comes in the form of her movie
of choice, which Bond superfans will instantly

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.600
recognize as "Moonraker."

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:12.170
There are some obvious parallels between "Moonraker"
and "Black Widow."

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:17.040
Both films feature an evil megalomaniac bent
on world domination, pulling strings from

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:21.900
a remote base in the sky — a space station
for "Moonraker" antagonist Hugo Drax and the

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:26.040
hovering Red Room installation of "Black Widow"
big bad Dreykov.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:31.140
Both primary antagonists also have a strong
and silent heavy — Drax's chief henchman

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:37.050
Jaws and Dreykov's daughter-turned-puppet
Antonia, aka Taskmaster — who only speaks

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:38.950
once, after their loyalty shifts.

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:46.409
When his sleeper cell-slash-family escapes
the United States to Cuba, Alexei is thrilled.

00:01:46.409 --> 00:01:50.590
After all, he was the Red Guardian, the Soviet
Union's first and only super soldier!

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:55.250
He had no business flying under the radar
in the American Midwest, stealing secrets;

00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:56.990
he belongs on the battlefront.

00:01:56.990 --> 00:01:59.190
"Ha ha ha!

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:00.659
Still fits!"

00:02:00.659 --> 00:02:05.890
When Natasha and Yelena rescue him from prison,
he describes their time as a faux family as

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:10.950
"tedious" and "boring," for which he tells
an obviously upset Yelena he means no offense

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:13.920
in deriding the only family she had ever known.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:18.189
As the movie goes on, Alexei's affections
for his fake daughters come out from behind

00:02:18.189 --> 00:02:21.150
the gruff and rigid exterior he shows the
world.

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:25.129
But viewers paying close attention to his
tattoos could have reached that conclusion

00:02:25.129 --> 00:02:26.260
much sooner.

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:30.390
Those two roses on his bicep, one of which
appears to have a drop of blood or tear falling

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:31.390
from it?

00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:35.209
It just so happens that the names on the ribbon
translate to Natasha and Yelena.

00:02:36.319 --> 00:02:41.499
As Natasha and Yelena are flying in to free
Alexei, their tablet showing the facility''s

00:02:41.499 --> 00:02:45.650
layout gives away his location: Seventh Circle
Prison.

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:49.480
Fans of certain Marvel Comics properties will
know that there's an eight-issue run of the

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:53.989
Daredevil-Punisher team-up series called "Seventh
Circle," but even though the Black Widow has

00:02:53.989 --> 00:02:58.730
been associated with both of those characters
previously, it's unlikely that's the intended

00:02:58.730 --> 00:02:59.730
reference.

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:04.029
It seems much more likely that it's a reference
to Dante's "Divine Comedy," more specifically,

00:03:04.029 --> 00:03:05.029
"Inferno."

00:03:05.029 --> 00:03:10.090
In Dante's 14th century poem, the seventh
circle of Hell is where sinners who've committed

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:13.439
violence are tortured by centaurs and harpies.

00:03:13.439 --> 00:03:17.980
Fans of classical Greek mythology may recall
harpies — loathsome, screeching creatures

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:22.109
with the head of a woman and the body of a
great bird — harassed Aeneas in Virgil's

00:03:22.109 --> 00:03:27.280
epic poem the "Aeneid", spoiling his crew's
food so they could not eat.

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:31.180
That sure sounds like a certain pair of prison
guards who've stolen the baked goods Alexei's

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:32.510
fans have sent him.

00:03:33.889 --> 00:03:37.999
Before his liberation from the Seventh Circle
Prison, Alexei regales his fellow inmates

00:03:37.999 --> 00:03:42.270
with the tales of his glory days as the Red
Guardian — Marvel Comics' Soviet-Russian

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:47.989
foil to Captain America — as he nonchalantly
takes on all comers in arm wrestling.

00:03:47.989 --> 00:03:52.340
It's clearly a tale they've heard before,
but one prisoner — credited as "Muscular

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:57.129
Gulag Inmate" to avoid spoilers — doesn't
buy Alexei's story, pointing out that Captain

00:03:57.129 --> 00:04:00.709
America was still frozen when the story supposedly
took place.

00:04:00.709 --> 00:04:05.080
Alexei isn't amused, asking "Are you calling
me a liar, Ursa?" before slamming the man's

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:06.809
arm to the table, breaking it.

00:04:06.809 --> 00:04:09.489
"Look at the big bear," he says mockingly.

00:04:09.489 --> 00:04:15.150
It looks like we just met Mikhail Ursus, aka
Ursa Major, a Soviet mutant who is able to

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:17.130
transform into a brown bear.

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:21.620
It's not the last time Alexei's heyday is
mocked; at Melina's farm, he tries to comfort

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:24.380
Yelena, only to have his concern rebuffed.

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:26.970
She says,
"The only thing you care about is your stupid

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:31.030
glory days as the Crimson Dynamo and no one
wants to hear about it."

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:35.120
It appears to be meant as an insult — with
Yelena either mocking his former code name

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:38.310
or actually forgetting it — which Alexei
corrects.

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:42.790
But Yelena has actually invoked yet another
Russian Marvel character, one we're unlikely

00:04:42.790 --> 00:04:46.220
to see in the MCU in a version faithful to
the source material.

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:47.220
Why?

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:53.250
Crimson Dynamo was the name Anton Vanko — father
to "Iron Man 2" villain Ivan Vanko, aka Whiplash

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:55.860
— took when wearing his own powered armor.

00:04:56.980 --> 00:05:01.850
As Yelena and Natasha are holed up in the
rafters of a Hungarian subway station, the

00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:05.220
two discuss the latter's previous time hiding
out in Budapest.

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:09.970
Nat's already made a reference to Clint Barton,
aka Hawkeye, in telling Yelena that the holes

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:13.150
in the safehouse walls were made by arrows,
not bullets.

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:17.480
She then reveals that she and Barton spent
two days hiding out there previously, at which

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:21.310
point the camera shows a series of games played
on a ventilation duct.

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:25.780
There are a few games of tic-tac-toe and a
game of Hangman, which someone seems to have

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:26.780
lost.

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:30.460
It seems to pretty clearly spell "butterfly,"
but it wouldn't make for much of an Easter

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:32.180
egg if it was that straightforward.

00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:36.240
These insects have been cropping up in the
MCU, with the butterflies in "WandaVision"

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:37.990
being the most recent example.

00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:43.530
Perhaps the most direct connection is to Layla
Miller, aka Butterfly, a Marvel Comics mutant

00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:48.190
with a broad range of abilities; one of her
powers is the ability to reanimate living

00:05:48.190 --> 00:05:50.750
things, but without restoring their souls.

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:56.140
It's an intriguing reference, given the exchange
on Vormir in Endgame, in which Natasha traded

00:05:56.140 --> 00:05:59.210
her life to get Clint the Soul Stone.

